  [1mClassification: bounded.[0m [38;5;153mformat()[39m already exists in [38;5;153mformat.js[39m with the same option-flag shape, and truncation is a
  choice of algorithm inside it — no new module or structure. So I'll present a short design in chat rather than write a
  spec. Say the word if you want the heavier treatment.

  Before I answer the exact-vs-word-boundary question, one thing decides the function's output contract and is awkward
  to change later:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m caps the [3mfinal returned string[0m (ellipsis included) or caps the [3mtext[0m with [38;5;153m'...'[39m appended on
  top. For [38;5;153mformat("hello world", { truncate: 8 })[39m that's [38;5;153m"hello..."[39m (8 chars out) versus [38;5;153m"hello wo..."[39m (11 chars out).

  - [1mEllipsis counts toward the limit[0m — the return value never exceeds [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, so callers sizing a column or a
    fixed-width cell can trust it. Costs you three characters of visible text, and needs a decision for [38;5;153mmaxLength < 3[39m.
  - [1mEllipsis appended on top[0m — simpler to implement and reason about ([38;5;153mstr.slice(0, max) + '...'[39m), preserves a full
    [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m of real text. But the returned string can be up to [38;5;153mmaxLength + 3[39m, so "max length" is a lie to any caller
    doing layout.

  [1mMy recommendation: ellipsis counts toward the limit.[0m The option is named for a maximum, and a maximum a caller can't
  rely on is the kind of thing that gets discovered by a broken layout rather than by a test. It's also the harder one
  to change later — flipping it afterward silently shifts every truncated output by three characters.

